actual, and avoid marketing fluff. Do **not** include your reasoning text\n   181→in the final answer—only the JSON.\n   182→\n   183→**File output + incremental updates requirement**\n   184→- Maintain a file named `new-product` in the current working directory.\n   185→- After every step (including each “Record progress, rewrite todo”), write the current JSON array to `new-product` (overwrite). Early steps may be partial/rough; later steps should be refined.\n   186→- The JSON must always be valid and reflect the best current view (start with `[]` if nothing yet).\n   187→- After the final step, ensure `new-product` holds the final sorted array.\n   188→\n   189→user:\n   190→Below is your QUESTION list. For each QUESTION, treat it as a separate “research lens”\n   191→to discover newly launched AI coding products (global constraint: last 14 days).\n   192→\n   193→1. QUESTION 1\n   194→   List newly launched AI coding tools from the last 14 days that position themselves as a “full AI IDE” (similar to Windsurf or Cursor), not just a small autocomplete plugin.\n   195→\n   196→2. QUESTION 2\n   197→   List newly launched AI coding CLIs or terminal-based agents from the last 14 days that behave like Gemini CLI, Codex CLI, or Claude Code CLI (repo-aware, can run commands, can manage tasks).\n   198→\n   199→3. QUESTION 5\n   200→   List newly launched AI tools from the last 14 days that focus on one specific developer workflow (for example debugging, refactoring, test generation, or migration) instead of being a general-purpose coding assistant.\n   201→\n   202→4. QUESTION 7\n   203→   List newly launched AI code agents from the last 14 days that advertise “project-long memory” or “workspace memory” across multiple sessions (not just single-chat context).\n   204→\n   205→5. QUESTION 11\n   206→   List newly launched open-source AI coding agents from the last 14 days that can be self-hosted or run locally by developers.\n   207→\n   208→6. QUESTION 14\n   209→   Lis